WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.758 --> 00:00:12.799
being assaulted, Fix finally stood up.

2
00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:17.842
He had an enormous bruise on his cheek, and his clothes were in tatters.

3
00:00:18.902 --> 00:00:20.863
Luckily, Aouda had escaped unharmed.

4
00:00:21.944 --> 00:00:24.345
Mr. Fogg was covered in dust, but he was fine.

5
00:00:25.566 --> 00:00:27.327
As soon as they were out of the crowd, Mr.

6
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:28.688
Fogg thanked the detective.

7
00:00:30.756 --> 00:00:33.138
"No thanks are necessary," replied Fix.

8
00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:34.779
"But let us go!"

9
00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:37.160
"Where?" asked Aouda.

10
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.741
"To a tailor!"

11
00:00:39.802 --> 00:00:42.023
He laughed, showing them his torn clothes.

12
00:00:42.924 --> 00:00:46.406
An hour later they returned to the hotel with Fix more suitably dressed.

13
00:00:48.127 --> 00:00:53.090
Passepartout was there, waiting for his master and armed with half a dozen six-barreled revolvers.

14
00:00:54.111 --> 00:00:56.092
When Passepartout saw Fix, he frowned.

15
00:00:56.768 --> 00:01:00.729
His expression changed, however, when Aouda explained their encounter with Colonel Proctor.

16
00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:05.671
"Fix seems to be keeping his word," Passepartout thought.

17
00:01:06.831 --> 00:01:09.852
At a quarter before six, the travelers reached the train station.

18
00:01:11.052 --> 00:01:14.933
It was already evening, cold and cheerless, and the sky was overcast.

19
00:01:15.993 --> 00:01:17.714
The train left the station on time.

20
00:01:19.014 --> 00:01:23.455
Even though it proceeded slowly, pausing at every stop along the line, the train kept Mr.

21
00:01:23.495 --> 00:01:25.116
Fogg and his companions on schedule.

22
00:01:26.664 --> 00:01:30.127
At eight o'clock, the steward entered the car and announced that it was time to go to bed.

23
00:01:31.348 --> 00:01:36.212
The backs of the seats were lowered, bedrolls were unpacked, and soon each traveler had a comfortable bed

24
00:01:36.252 --> 00:01:37.993
with clean sheets and a soft pillow.

25
00:01:40.195 --> 00:01:42.456
The next morning, after eating breakfast, Mr.

26
00:01:42.496 --> 00:01:46.179
Fogg and his companions watched the landscape of the vast prairies.

27
00:01:47.841 --> 00:01:51.824
Mountains lined the horizon, and creeks and rivers passed by their windows.

28
00:01:52.904 --> 00:01:57.767
Around noon the train approached a herd of ten—or twelve—thousand buffalo that were crossing the tracks.

29
00:01:58.807 --> 00:02:02.990
The train slowed, attempting to push through, but the mass of animals was too great.

30
00:02:04.771 --> 00:02:07.692
It took three full hours for the beasts to clear the tracks.

31
00:02:09.653 --> 00:02:14.136
By the time the last buffalo crossed the rails, the first had already reached the horizon.

32
00:02:15.236 --> 00:02:17.077
Passepartout was furious at the delay.

33
00:02:17.097 --> 00:02:18.778
"What a country!"

34
00:02:19.118 --> 00:02:19.639
he cried.

35
00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:21.900
"Mere cattle stop the trains.

36
00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:23.749
I would like to know if Mr.

37
00:02:23.789 --> 00:02:25.009
Fogg foresaw this delay!"

38
00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:31.173
While Passepartout was cursing impatiently, Aouda was growing anxious for a different reason.

39
00:02:32.214 --> 00:02:36.316
At the last stop, several passengers had walked up and down the platform for fresh air.

40
00:02:37.276 --> 00:02:39.778
Among them Aouda had recognized Colonel Proctor.

41
00:02:40.758 --> 00:02:42.860
Her heart pounded when she saw the man that Mr.

42
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.200
Fogg had promised to challenge.

43
00:02:45.861 --> 00:02:48.343
It was necessary, at all costs, to prevent Mr.

44
00:02:48.383 --> 00:02:49.984
Fogg from seeing his adversary.

45
00:02:51.068 --> 00:02:52.608
Aouda seized a moment when Mr.

46
00:02:52.648 --> 00:02:55.009
Fogg was asleep to tell Fix and Passepartout.

47
00:02:55.029 --> 00:02:57.990
"Colonel Proctor on this train!"

48
00:02:58.330 --> 00:02:58.950
cried Fix.

49
00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.991
"Well, before he settles with Mr.

50
00:03:02.031 --> 00:03:03.872
Fogg, he has got to settle with me!"

51
00:03:03.892 --> 00:03:07.212
"Mr. Fix," replied Aouda, "Mr.

52
00:03:07.252 --> 00:03:09.033
Fogg will allow no one to avenge him.

53
00:03:10.053 --> 00:03:14.014
He told me that he was prepared to return to America after his journey to find this man.

54
00:03:14.954 --> 00:03:16.595
He must not see Colonel Proctor!"

55
00:03:17.475 --> 00:03:20.336
Mr. Fogg woke up, and the conversation quickly stopped.

56
00:03:21.364 --> 00:03:23.665
Aouda madly searched for a way to distract Mr.

57
00:03:23.705 --> 00:03:27.106
Fogg. Suddenly an idea came to her: whist!

58
00:03:28.247 --> 00:03:31.308
She whispered her idea to Fix, and soon after a game began.

59
00:03:32.509 --> 00:03:35.870
Fix knew the game well, and Aouda and Passepartout picked it up quickly.

60
00:03:36.691 --> 00:03:37.731
They played all morning.

61
00:03:39.872 --> 00:03:43.794
Shortly after lunch they had just begun a new round when the train let out a whistle and

62
00:03:43.854 --> 00:03:49.396
stopped. Passepartout put his head out the window but could see nothing that could have caused the delay.

63
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.761
He jumped out of the car and found the signalman talking to the engineer.

64
00:03:53.781 --> 00:03:57.542
"No! You can't pass," the signalman was saying.

65
00:03:57.562 --> 00:04:01.384
"The bridge at Medicine Creek is shaky and can't bear the weight of the train!

66
00:04:02.264 --> 00:04:03.124
The river is deep there.

67
00:04:03.664 --> 00:04:04.304
It's not safe."

68
00:04:05.385 --> 00:04:09.446
The passengers would have to walk along the river to a safe place to cross and then walk

69
00:04:09.486 --> 00:04:11.347
another six hours to the next station.

70
00:04:12.387 --> 00:04:15.168
Passepartout's heart almost stopped when he heard this news.

71
00:04:17.376 --> 00:04:21.479
By now a crowd had gathered around the engineer and the signalman, and an argument broke out.

72
00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.322
Finally the engineer calmed the crowd and suggested a daring plan.

73
00:04:27.163 --> 00:04:31.806
By accelerating the train to its very highest speed, they might have a chance of racing over the

74
00:04:31.846 --> 00:04:34.127
bridge before it collapsed into the rapids below.

75
00:04:36.529 --> 00:04:37.750
Passepartout was shocked.

76
00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.733
He was ready to try almost anything to get over Medicine Creek, but this solution was almost a

77
00:04:42.773 --> 00:04:43.674
little too daring!

78
00:04:44.554 --> 00:04:46.996
He ran back to the cabin to tell the others what was happening.

79
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.361
"All aboard!"

80
00:04:49.661 --> 00:04:50.482
cried the conductor.

81
00:04:52.203 --> 00:04:57.166
The engineer reversed the train for almost a mile, and then, with a whistle, the train began moving

82
00:04:57.246 --> 00:04:57.827
forward again.

83
00:04:58.688 --> 00:05:03.851
The engine puffed huge clouds of steam as the train gained speed, going faster and faster.

84
00:05:04.892 --> 00:05:06.913
The cars shook and the windows rattled.

85
00:05:08.495 --> 00:05:11.597
It was like a frightful iron beast, racing to its destruction.

86
00:05:12.698 --> 00:05:15.780
Just before the river, the train exceeded a hundred miles an hour.

87
00:05:16.948 --> 00:05:19.549
It sailed over the bridge, hardly touching the rails at all!

88
00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:25.192
The last car had just reached the other side safely when the bridge fell with a crash into

89
00:05:25.212 --> 00:05:26.052
the rapids below.

90
00:05:31.835 --> 00:05:37.678
That was quite a train ride—being delayed by thousands of buffalo, speeding over a collapsing bridge Whew!

91
00:05:38.918 --> 00:05:41.800
Aside from these incidents, our train journey has been quite enjoyable.

92
00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:47.412
We have been traveling over America's prairies, large areas of open land like fields or meadows.

93
00:05:48.553 --> 00:05:52.496
Tall grass and colorful wildflowers grow on the prairies, but there are very few trees.

94
00:05:53.958 --> 00:05:58.341
In rainy areas the grass grows very tall, so tall that you could ride a horse and no

95
00:05:58.401 --> 00:05:58.982
one would see you!

96
00:05:59.783 --> 00:06:00.663
Now that's tall!

97
00:06:02.165 --> 00:06:05.868
Many kinds of animals live here, including antelope, buffalo, and rabbits.

98
00:06:06.829 --> 00:06:10.692
Buffalo are the largest mammals in North America, standing close to six feet high.

99
00:06:11.668 --> 00:06:15.432
Even though they're big, they can run really fast, about 25 miles an hour.

100
00:06:16.353 --> 00:06:18.295
So don't tease a buffalo because it'll catch you.

101
00:06:19.276 --> 00:06:20.438
And buffalo have horns.

102
00:06:21.218 --> 00:06:21.439
Ouch!
